# Problem memo -- The Green Thumb (Lily Heeren, Lucas Jenkins)

## The user
Our device can be used by any person who is a household plant owner and would like to monitor their plants for better care of them. When it comes to the place, our device can be used in any household that holds plants and would like to monitor them more deeply.

## The problem
The problem we are trying to resolve with our device is incorrect care of plants. Household plants can be quite expensive and time consuming. While most people might think plants are easy to keep alive, a lot of times they can be over-watered, get the incorrect amount of sun, or overall not live as long as they could because of how they are cared for. These problems can happen a lot and the main cost is monetary, time, and the life of the plants themselves.

## Why a device
This device is a good idea because we would be able to monitor and report about a plant at all times, this would allow the user to not constantly monitor their plant and overall give their plants better lifespans. A phone app can not reasonably solve the problem we have and the hardware is essential so we can monitor the stats of the plants directly.

## The sensors
The sensors we plan to use in our project are light and moisture sensors. The light sensors will allow us to monitor how much sunlight the plant is receiving and the moisture sensor will allow us to monitor when the plant will need water, or if it is being over-wattered. We also may decide to use temperature sensors to read what temperature the plant and surrounding area is for optimal plant life. These cooperate with our device as they are essential to monitoring the health and needs of the plant.

## The mechanisms
The first mechanism that we plan to build from the list is mechanism D becuase the memory problem is a high potential if our device suddly stops or looses power, the ability to not loose information when that happens is important to the success of our project. The second mechanism we plan to build is mechanism E becuase splitting our problem up into more self-suffiecnt pieces is better for the overall outcoem of our project and will allow us to test more effiecntly.

## The risk
Our software heavily relies on the data we gather from our device, so if one piece of the data or software is slightly off then we could have massive problems. Overall, our most likely sink is poor execution of software with the data collected.
# CS370: Term Project
For Colorado State University's Operating Systems course, we were tasked with creating a term project that would address a real-world problem and present a solution that isn't easily solved with an application. This project suggests utilizing a low cost, single-board computer (Raspberry Pi) with a minimum of two sensors.

## Our Project: The Plant Owner's Capable Assistant (in-progress)
For our project, we are creating a mini houseplant assistant that enables a user to give their plants the best care possible by measuring soil-moisture and light exposure. We believe this project is not easily solved by an app, and that current similar products on the market, have shown common gaps in their capabilities. Thus, we want to create an effective solution that will help bridge the communication gap between plants and humans.

# make predefines CC=cc, so `?=` never fires; only replace make's default.
ifeq ($(origin CC),default)
CC := gcc
endif
BUILD ?= build
BASE_CFLAGS := -std=c17 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -Wall -Wextra -Werror -g -O1 -Iinclude -Itests -pthread
CFLAGS := $(BASE_CFLAGS) $(EXTRA_CFLAGS)

# Library sources are everything in src/ (process main()s should live in
# src/<name>_main.c and be listed in MAIN_SRC so tests don't link them).
MAIN_SRC := $(wildcard src/*_main.c)
LIB_SRC := $(filter-out $(MAIN_SRC),$(wildcard src/*.c))
HEADERS := $(wildcard include/*.h tests/*.h)

# Every tests/test_*.c becomes $(BUILD)/test_* linked against all of LIB_SRC.
TEST_SRC := $(wildcard tests/test_*.c)
TEST_BINS := $(patsubst tests/%.c,$(BUILD)/%,$(TEST_SRC))

VALGRIND := valgrind --leak-check=full --show-leak-kinds=all --error-exitcode=1

all: $(TEST_BINS) $(BUILD)/light_probe

$(BUILD)/%: tests/%.c $(LIB_SRC) $(HEADERS)
	@mkdir -p $(BUILD)
	$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $(LIB_SRC) $< -o $@

# On-Pi BH1750 bring-up check (see src/light_probe_main.c). Not run by
# `make test`: it needs real I2C hardware.
$(BUILD)/light_probe: src/light_probe_main.c $(LIB_SRC) $(HEADERS)
	@mkdir -p $(BUILD)
	$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $(LIB_SRC) $< -o $@

light_probe: $(BUILD)/light_probe

test: $(TEST_BINS)
	@for b in $(TEST_BINS); do echo "== $$b"; ./$$b || exit 1; done

# Sanitizers get their own build dirs so they never reuse plain objects.
asan:
	$(MAKE) test BUILD=build/asan \
	  EXTRA_CFLAGS="-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer"

ubsan:
	$(MAKE) test BUILD=build/ubsan \
	  EXTRA_CFLAGS="-fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined"

# valgrind and ASan don't mix, so memcheck runs the plain build.
memcheck: $(TEST_BINS)
	@for b in $(TEST_BINS); do echo "== valgrind $$b"; $(VALGRIND) ./$$b || exit 1; done

clean:
	rm -rf build

.PHONY: all test asan ubsan memcheck clean light_probe
name: C Tests

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  test:
    name: Tests
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Run tests
        run: make test

  asan:
    name: ASan + UBSan
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Run sanitizers
        run: make asan

  memcheck:
    name: Valgrind Memcheck
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Install Valgrind
        run: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y valgrind

      - name: Run Memcheck
        run: make memcheck
#ifndef HUB_H
#define HUB_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include "ipc.h"

typedef struct gt_hub gt_hub;

gt_hub   *gt_hub_create(void);
/* Validate a message, stamp seq + time, and fill `out` for forwarding. */
gt_status gt_hub_accept(gt_hub *h, const gt_msg *in, uint64_t now_us, gt_msg *out);
/* Per-sensor last-seen check; returns number of timed-out sensors. */
int       gt_hub_check_timeouts(gt_hub *h, uint64_t now_us);
void      gt_hub_destroy(gt_hub *h);

#endif
#ifndef I2C_BUS_H
#define I2C_BUS_H

#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include "ipc.h"

/* Hardware seam: drivers talk to the bus only through these ops, so tests
 * can swap in a fake device instead of /dev/i2c-1. */
typedef struct {
    gt_status (*write)(void *ctx, const uint8_t *buf, size_t len);
    gt_status (*read)(void *ctx, uint8_t *buf, size_t len);   /* short read => GT_ERR_IO */
    void      (*sleep_ms)(void *ctx, uint32_t ms);             /* injectable so tests don't sleep */
    void      (*close)(void *ctx);
} gt_i2c_ops;

typedef struct {
    const gt_i2c_ops *ops;
    void             *ctx;
} gt_i2c_bus;

/* Linux impl: open(dev, O_RDWR) + ioctl(I2C_SLAVE, addr). dev: e.g. "/dev/i2c-1" */
gt_status gt_i2c_linux_open(const char *dev, uint8_t addr, gt_i2c_bus *out);

#endif
#ifndef IPC_H
#define IPC_H

#include <stdint.h>
/* Shared types: sensor IDs (IPC message and on-disk record) and status codes. */
typedef enum {
    GT_SENSOR_ALL   = 0,
    GT_SENSOR_LIGHT = 1,
    GT_SENSOR_SOIL  = 2
} gt_sensor_id;

/* 0 on success, negative on failure. */
typedef enum {
    GT_OK          =  0,
    GT_ERR_IO      = -1,
    GT_ERR_INVAL   = -2,
    GT_ERR_NOMEM   = -3,
    GT_ERR_CORRUPT = -4,
    GT_ERR_FULL    = -5
} gt_status;

/* 16 B sensor -> hub -> storage message over AF_UNIX sockets in /run/plantpi. */
#define GT_MSG_SIZE 16

typedef struct {
    uint8_t  sensor_id;   /* gt_sensor_id */
    uint32_t counter;     /* sensor-side counter, used to detect gaps */
    uint32_t seq;         /* hub-assigned; 0 before the hub stamps it */
    uint32_t value;       /* raw count */
} gt_msg;

gt_status gt_msg_encode(const gt_msg *m, uint8_t out[GT_MSG_SIZE]);
gt_status gt_msg_decode(const uint8_t in[GT_MSG_SIZE], gt_msg *m);

/* Client side: reconnect with 100 ms -> 2 s backoff; bounded send queue. */
typedef struct gt_ipc_client gt_ipc_client;

gt_ipc_client *gt_ipc_client_open(const char *sock_path);
gt_status      gt_ipc_client_send(gt_ipc_client *c, const gt_msg *m);
void           gt_ipc_client_close(gt_ipc_client *c);

#endif
#ifndef LIGHT_SENSOR_H
#define LIGHT_SENSOR_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include "i2c_bus.h"
#include "ipc.h"

#define GT_LIGHT_I2C_ADDR 0x23   /* BH1750 */

/* BH1750 opcodes and timing (datasheet). */
#define GT_BH1750_POWER_DOWN   0x00
#define GT_BH1750_POWER_ON     0x01
#define GT_BH1750_RESET        0x07   /* clears data register; only valid while powered on */
#define GT_BH1750_CONT_HRES    0x10   /* continuous high-res mode, 1 lx/count */
#define GT_BH1750_MEAS_MAX_MS  180    /* worst-case high-res conversion time */
#define GT_LIGHT_RETRIES       3      /* extra read attempts after a failure */
#define GT_LIGHT_RETRY_GAP_MS  10

typedef struct gt_light_sensor gt_light_sensor;

/* Raw counts are what get stored; convert only for display/analysis. */
static inline double gt_light_raw_to_lux(uint32_t raw) { return raw / 1.2; }

/* i2c_dev: e.g. "/dev/i2c-1" */
gt_light_sensor *gt_light_open(const char *i2c_dev);
/* Takes ownership of `bus` (closed on failure or by gt_light_close). */
gt_light_sensor *gt_light_open_bus(gt_i2c_bus bus);
gt_status        gt_light_read(gt_light_sensor *s, uint32_t *raw_out);
void             gt_light_close(gt_light_sensor *s);

#endif
#ifndef LOG_RECORD_H
#define LOG_RECORD_H

#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include "ipc.h"

/* On-disk format: see DESIGN.md "In what format". Little-endian, packed. */
#define GT_SEG_MAGIC      "GTLG"
#define GT_SEG_VERSION    1
#define GT_SEG_HEADER_SZ  16
#define GT_REC_MAGIC      0xA7
#define GT_REC_VERSION    1
#define GT_REC_SIZE       24

typedef enum { GT_REC_DATA = 0x01, GT_REC_GAP = 0x02 } gt_rec_type;

typedef struct {
    uint8_t  type;       /* gt_rec_type */
    uint8_t  sensor_id;  /* gt_sensor_id */
    uint32_t seq;
    uint64_t timestamp_us;
    uint32_t value;      /* DATA: raw count; GAP: refused-reading count */
} gt_record;

uint32_t  gt_crc32(const void *buf, size_t len);
gt_status gt_record_encode(const gt_record *rec, uint8_t out[GT_REC_SIZE]);
gt_status gt_record_decode(const uint8_t in[GT_REC_SIZE], gt_record *rec);

#endif
#ifndef SAMPLER_H
#define SAMPLER_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include "ipc.h"

/* Callbacks so the schedule can be tested without I2C hardware. */
typedef gt_status (*gt_read_fn)(void *ctx, uint32_t *raw_out);
typedef gt_status (*gt_emit_fn)(void *ctx, gt_sensor_id id, uint32_t raw);

typedef struct {
    gt_sensor_id id;
    uint32_t     period_ms;   /* 1000 light, 60000 soil */
    gt_read_fn   read;
    void        *read_ctx;
} gt_sampler_cfg;

typedef struct gt_sampler gt_sampler;

gt_sampler *gt_sampler_create(const gt_sampler_cfg *cfg, gt_emit_fn emit, void *emit_ctx);
/* One tick against a monotonic clock in ms; reads and emits if due. */
gt_status   gt_sampler_tick(gt_sampler *s, uint64_t now_ms);
void        gt_sampler_destroy(gt_sampler *s);

#endif
#ifndef SOIL_SENSOR_H
#define SOIL_SENSOR_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include "ipc.h"

#define GT_SOIL_I2C_ADDR 0x36    /* Adafruit STEMMA (seesaw) */

typedef struct gt_soil_sensor gt_soil_sensor;

/* i2c_dev: e.g. "/dev/i2c-1" */
gt_soil_sensor *gt_soil_open(const char *i2c_dev);
gt_status       gt_soil_read(gt_soil_sensor *s, uint32_t *raw_out);
void            gt_soil_close(gt_soil_sensor *s);

#endif
#ifndef STORAGE_H
#define STORAGE_H

#include <stddef.h>
#include "ipc.h"
#include "log_record.h"

#define GT_BATCH_MAX_RECORDS 64   /* N */
#define GT_BATCH_MAX_AGE_S   60   /* T */

typedef struct gt_storage gt_storage;

/* Opens the segment dir and runs recovery before accepting data. */
gt_storage *gt_storage_open(const char *dir);
/* Scan one segment; truncate at the first bad record. */
gt_status   gt_storage_recover(const char *segment_path, size_t *removed_records);
gt_status   gt_storage_append(gt_storage *s, const gt_record *rec);
/* write() + fsync() the batch; a record is committed once this returns OK. */
gt_status   gt_storage_flush(gt_storage *s);
void        gt_storage_close(gt_storage *s);

#endif
#ifndef SUPERVISOR_H
#define SUPERVISOR_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include "ipc.h"

typedef struct gt_supervisor gt_supervisor;

gt_supervisor *gt_sup_create(void);
/* Register a child by argv; started in dependency order. */
gt_status      gt_sup_add_child(gt_supervisor *s, const char *name, char *const argv[]);
/* One poll() iteration: reap SIGCHLD, check heartbeats, restart with backoff. */
gt_status      gt_sup_step(gt_supervisor *s, uint64_t now_ms);
void           gt_sup_destroy(gt_supervisor *s);

#endif

